Relationship with the Pigsty RPM packages
-----------------------------------------
This package and the Pigsty HAProxy RPM packages share one runtime contract.
The baseline configuration, the systemd unit, /etc/default/haproxy, the
logrotate rule, the rsyslog snippet, the tmpfiles definition and the shipped
error pages all come from a single haproxy-utils archive and are installed byte
for byte identically on every supported distribution.

Distribution native mechanisms are deliberately kept: this package still uses
adduser, a SysV init script, a vim addon, and Debian's HTML documentation, and
it still ships the error pages under /etc/haproxy/errors so that existing
configurations keep working. /usr/share/haproxy holds the same pages and is the
path that also exists on the RPM side.

Default logging
---------------
The package baseline logs to standard output. Under systemd, messages are
captured by the service manager and are available through:

    journalctl -u haproxy

This default does not require a syslog daemon and remains usable after HAProxy
enters its chroot.

Optional syslog integration
---------------------------
To route HAProxy logs through syslog instead, replace the global stdout target
with an appropriate local target, for example:

    global
        log /dev/log local0

The package ships an optional rsyslog snippet that routes HAProxy messages to
/var/log/haproxy.log and provides the chroot socket for SysV operation. Under
systemd, the vendor unit also makes the host /dev/log available inside the
default chroot. Reload or restart rsyslog after installing the package if you
use that integration. Other syslog implementations require their own routing
rules and chroot socket handling.

No default listener
-------------------
The package baseline ships no network listener. The only open interface is the
local runtime API socket described below: the service starts, validates, and
reloads cleanly while it waits for a deployment to add complete listener
sections under /etc/haproxy/conf.d. Statistics pages, health endpoints, and
the built-in Prometheus exporter are deployment concerns and belong in those
listener sections.

Runtime API
-----------
The baseline also opens the HAProxy runtime API on a local socket:

    /run/haproxy/admin.sock

The socket is mode 660. Its directory is 2775 haproxy:haproxy, so the socket
created by the master process before it drops privileges ends up owned by the
haproxy group. Only root and members of that group can use it, for example
through the packaged helper:

    haproxy-reload

That helper needs socat, which this package recommends.

Connection limit
----------------
The baseline sets an explicit "maxconn 8192". Without it HAProxy derives the
limit from the host file descriptor limit, which differs between distributions
and would make both the connection ceiling and the resident memory footprint
depend on the systemd defaults of the machine. Raise it in your own
configuration if the deployment needs more.

Upgrades
--------
Package upgrades reload the running instance rather than restarting it. The
reload path validates the configuration and then signals the master process to
re-execute, so the new binary is picked up while established connections drain
on the previous workers. A stopped service stays stopped.

Changing EXTRAOPTS in /etc/default/haproxy is the exception: process arguments
are only re-read on a full restart. Note also that EXTRAOPTS replaces the unit
default outright, so keep -S /run/haproxy-master.sock unless you intend to
disable the master CLI socket.

Memory allocator
----------------
Since 3.4.3-2PIGSTY this package links against the system allocator instead of
jemalloc, matching the Pigsty RPM packages.

jemalloc is a supported option upstream and measurably lowers CPU use on
connection churn heavy workloads, at the cost of roughly 40 MB of additional
resident memory per process. It was dropped here because libjemalloc is only
available from EPEL on Enterprise Linux, so keeping it would either add an
EPEL runtime dependency to a component installed on every node, or leave the
two package families with different memory and performance behaviour. For the
database proxying workload these packages target, where connections are long
lived and the connection rate is low, that trade did not pay for itself.

Rebuild with -ljemalloc in ADDLIB if your workload is allocator bound.
